<div class="tldr"><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> every person in the backcountry needs three pieces of avalanche rescue gear: a transceiver (beacon), a probe, and a shovel, plus the training to use them fast. This gear helps you find and dig out a buried partner. It does not prevent avalanches. Take a recognized avalanche course, check the local avalanche forecast every day, and practice searches regularly.</p></div>

<h2>Why the gear works only as a team</h2>
<p>If someone is buried, the people nearby are the rescue. Outside help usually can't arrive in time, which is why every member of a group carries the same gear and knows how to use it. The three tools work together:</p>
<table>
  <caption>The three essential pieces of avalanche rescue gear</caption>
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Gear</th><th scope="col">What it does</th><th scope="col">When it's used</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><th scope="row">Transceiver (beacon)</th><td>Sends a signal while you travel; switches to search mode to locate a buried person</td><td>Worn on your body, switched on, every time you're in avalanche terrain</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Probe</th><td>A collapsible pole that pinpoints a buried person's exact location and depth</td><td>After the beacon search narrows the area</td></tr>
    <tr><th scope="row">Shovel</th><td>A sturdy collapsible shovel for digging through dense avalanche debris</td><td>Immediately after a probe strike</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Leaving any one of them at home breaks the system. A beacon without a probe and shovel can find someone you can't dig out.</p>

<h2>Avalanche transceiver (beacon)</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Wear it on your body,</strong> under a layer, in a harness or a secure pocket designed for it, not in your pack.</li>
  <li><strong>Turn it on and check it</strong> at the trailhead. Groups do a beacon check to confirm every unit sends and receives.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose a modern digital transceiver with multiple antennas,</strong> which most current models are. Features differ, so learn the one you own thoroughly.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep fresh batteries</strong> and follow the manufacturer's guidance on battery type and firmware updates.</li>
</ul>
<p>Browse <%= gear_link "avalanche beacons", "avalanche beacon" %>.</p>

<h2>Probe</h2>
<p>Look for a probe long enough for deep debris and quick to assemble with gloves on. Practice assembling it until it's automatic. Browse <%= gear_link "avalanche probes", "avalanche probe" %>.</p>

<h2>Shovel</h2>
<p>Avalanche debris sets up hard, so choose a sturdy metal blade rather than a plastic one, with a handle that extends for leverage. Learning an efficient digging technique as a group often matters as much as the shovel itself. Browse <%= gear_link "avalanche shovels", "avalanche shovel" %>.</p>

<h2>How a companion rescue works</h2>
<p>An avalanche course teaches this in detail with hands-on practice. The broad sequence:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Make sure the scene is safe</strong> for rescuers before going in, and watch for further slides.</li>
  <li><strong>Note where the person was last seen.</strong> That narrows the search area.</li>
  <li><strong>Switch every rescuer's beacon to search,</strong> so other transmitting units don't confuse the search.</li>
  <li><strong>Search with beacons,</strong> following the signal from a rough search to a fine search close to the surface.</li>
  <li><strong>Probe to pinpoint</strong> the buried person, leaving the probe in place after a strike.</li>
  <li><strong>Dig strategically,</strong> starting downhill of the probe and moving snow efficiently as a team.</li>
  <li><strong>Call for help and give first aid</strong> as soon as the person is reached.</li>
</ol>
<p>Every step depends on practice. Rescuers who have never run a drill lose time at each stage.</p>

<h2>Checking and caring for your gear</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Before every season:</strong> inspect your probe sections and cable, check your shovel blade and handle for cracks, and test your beacon.</li>
  <li><strong>Before every trip:</strong> confirm battery level and do a group beacon check.</li>
  <li><strong>Pack it where you can reach it fast.</strong> Many packs have a dedicated pocket for the probe and shovel.</li>
  <li><strong>Keep electronics away from your beacon</strong> as the manufacturer recommends, since phones and other devices can interfere with the signal.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Airbag packs</h2>
<p>Avalanche airbag packs inflate a large airbag when triggered, intended to help keep the wearer closer to the surface of a moving avalanche. They are an additional tool, not a replacement for a beacon, probe, and shovel, and they don't make risky terrain safe. If you carry one, learn its trigger system and any travel rules that apply to its canister or battery.</p>

<h2>Buying or renting your first set</h2>
<p>If you're just starting out, renting a beacon, probe, and shovel for your avalanche course is a sensible way to try gear before you buy. Many mountain shops and guide services rent rescue gear. When you buy, a few practical points help:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pick a beacon you find intuitive.</strong> Simple displays and clear directional cues matter most under stress.</li>
  <li><strong>Match your partners where you can,</strong> or at least make sure everyone knows how each other's beacon works.</li>
  <li><strong>Buy from a reputable retailer</strong> and avoid used beacons with an unknown history or outdated firmware.</li>
  <li><strong>Make sure everything fits in your pack</strong> and comes out fast.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Training matters more than any gear</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Take a recognized avalanche course.</strong> In the US, look for AIARE or American Avalanche Association (AAA) courses; in Canada, Avalanche Canada training courses. Courses cover avalanche terrain, the daily forecast, decision-making, and companion rescue.</li>
  <li><strong>Check your regional avalanche forecast every day</strong> you go out. US centers are listed at <a href="https://avalanche.org" rel="nofollow noopener">avalanche.org</a>; Canada's forecasts are at <a href="https://avalanche.ca" rel="nofollow noopener">avalanche.ca</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Practice beacon searches</strong> several times a season, including scenarios with more than one burial. Many areas have beacon training parks.</li>
  <li><strong>Travel with trained partners</strong> and hire a certified guide when you're starting out.</li>
</ul>
<p>As general education, avalanches commonly release on slopes of roughly 30 to 45 degrees, and you can be caught from below in a runout zone. Courses teach how to recognize this terrain and choose safer routes based on conditions.</p>

<h2>Where this applies</h2>
<p>Any time you're outside controlled resort terrain: backcountry ski tours, <%= link_to "splitboarding", guide_path("splitboarding") %>, snowshoeing or hiking through avalanche terrain, and resort sidecountry accessed through boundary gates. Terrain beyond a resort boundary is uncontrolled, even if it's a short traverse from the lifts. Starting out? Read <%= link_to "backcountry skiing for beginners", guide_path("backcountry-skiing") %>.</p>
<% faqs << { question: "What avalanche gear do I need?", answer: "Each person needs an avalanche transceiver (beacon), a probe, and a shovel, plus training to use them. An airbag pack can be an additional tool, but it doesn't replace the three essentials." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Does an avalanche beacon keep me safe?", answer: "No. A beacon helps partners find you if you're buried, and helps you find them. It doesn't prevent avalanches. Avalanche education, the daily forecast, and careful terrain choices are what reduce risk." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Where should I wear my avalanche beacon?", answer: "On your body, under a layer, in a harness or a secure pocket designed for it, switched on before you enter avalanche terrain. Don't keep it in your backpack." } %>
<% faqs << { question: "Where can I take an avalanche course?", answer: "In the US, look for courses following the AIARE or American Avalanche Association curriculum; in Canada, look for Avalanche Canada training courses. Regional avalanche centers often list local providers." } %>
